C LB+ctrl
"Self-fulfilling speculation: P0 reads x, and if it sees 0 writes 1 to y; P1
 reads y, and if it sees 0 writes 1 to x. The Theory of Speculative Computation
 (TSC, Boudol & Petri, ESOP 2010) places NO restriction on speculation, so it
 PERMITS the self-fulfilling outcome (it explicitly admits this thin-air shape,
 citing the Java spec). C11 also permits this relaxed control-dependent LB, so
 OOTA does NOT separate them -- correcting the naive assumption that TSC forbids
 thin-air. The real asymmetry is about DRF (see README): under unrestricted
 speculation TSC's EXTERNAL DRF theorem FAILS (a race-free-under-interleaving
 program can show a non-SC outcome), whereas C11 forces DRF programs to behave
 SC; internal DRF holds for both. TSC is a lambda/imperative calculus with locks
 and no relaxed/release/acquire modes, so there is no herd7-runnable C11-vs-TSC
 litmus -- both verdicts are CITED / model-level. This file documents the
 speculative shape only."
{ int x=0; int y=0; }
P0 (atomic_int* x, atomic_int* y) {
  int r1 = atomic_load_explicit(x,memory_order_relaxed);
  if (r1 == 0) atomic_store_explicit(y,1,memory_order_relaxed);
}
P1 (atomic_int* x, atomic_int* y) {
  int r2 = atomic_load_explicit(y,memory_order_relaxed);
  if (r2 == 0) atomic_store_explicit(x,1,memory_order_relaxed);
}
exists (0:r1=0 /\ 1:r2=0 /\ x=1 /\ y=1)
